                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Frustratingly cryptic detective work bogged down by obtuse progression.\r\n\r\nIf you are thinking of picking up The Testament of Sherlock Holmes, it is a visually ambitious title that captures a fantastic 2012 atmosphere, but it is far from accessible. While the environmental design and strong narrative baseline show massive potential, the game forces an overly convoluted gameplay loop that will thoroughly test your patience.\r\n\r\nHere is what you need to know before diving in:\r\n\r\n• Cryptic Progression Barriers: The game suffers from incredibly opaque step-by-step requirements just to advance a case. Unlike modern entries in the franchise that gently guide you toward your next logical goal, this title frequently stalls your momentum with overly complex, unexplained tasks that leave you entirely unsure of how to proceed.\r\n\r\n• Alt-Tab \"Gameplay\": The lack of clarity in both the environment and the user interface means you will likely spend more time staring at an alternative screen than playing the actual game. To get any meaningful investigation going, having a secondary window or mobile browser open to look up guides, walkthroughs, or community solutions becomes a mandatory chore.\r\n\r\n• Impressive 2012 Presentation: The asset generation and atmospheric lighting effects are genuinely great for the year it was released. Frogwares managed to craft a highly compelling, dark, and visually impressive aesthetic for Victorian London that remains one of the high points of the experience.\r\n\r\n• Unforgiving Puzzle Design: The title leans heavily into relentless \"moon logic\" riddles and mechanical puzzles that offer zero explanation. If you are coming backward from the modern, streamlined Sherlock Holmes games, the steep difficulty spikes and poorly explained UI elements make solving mysteries yourself feel completely out of reach.\r\n\r\nFinal Verdict: An interesting piece of Frogwares history that is simply ruined by poor execution and a lack of mechanical clarity. If you do not want to constantly seek out external sources just to finish a single case, do yourself a huge favor and avoid this one—stick to the franchise's more refined, modern successors instead.",
